/E (      In the early stages, this loneliness is often misunderstood. It can feel like something important is missing, like joy, connection, or spontaneity have been sacrificed. But in reality, what is being removed is not meaning, it is noise. The constant stimulation, the endless distractions, the automatic behaviors that once filled time without offering direction slowly fade, and in their absence, silence appears. That silence can be unsettling, especially in a world where being alone with your thoughts is no longer common or encouraged. Discipline forces a person to spend more time with themselves, without the usual escapes, without constant reassurance from others, and without the comforting illusion that staying busy is the same as moving forward. This is not easy. It exposes doubts, insecurities, and unanswered questions that were previously masked by routine or distraction. Yet this exposure is not a weakness; it is a necessary stage of growth. When distractions fall away, truth has space to surface, and clarity begins to form not from external advice, but from honest self-observation.)
/Pg 62 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
