/E (     Disciplined people are often misunderstood because their behavior does not align with what modern culture expects from engagement, ambition, or success. In a world that values constant availability, emotional display, and visible effort, discipline tends to look distant, rigid, or even cold. What is frequently interpreted as lack of passion or empathy is, in reality, a deliberate choice to conserve energy and attention for what truly matters.)
/Pg 70 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1519 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 965 0 R
/E (     One source of misunderstanding comes from contrast. Disciplined individuals often move at a different pace. They respond less, explain less, and react more slowly, not because they are indifferent, but because they have learned that not every stimulus deserves a response. For those accustomed to immediacy, this restraint can feel like rejection or superiority, even when it is neither. Discipline also disrupts familiar social patterns. When someone consistently keeps boundaries around time, habits, and priorities, it can unintentionally challenge others to reflect on their own choices. This discomfort is rarely acknowledged directly, but it often expresses itself as criticism. The disciplined person is labeled extreme, obsessive or disconnected, when in fact they are simply no longer participating in patterns that drain them.)
/Pg 70 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
