/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 971 0 R
/T (    However, there is a crucial warning: delegation of responsabilities is not advisable for those who are still developing the essential skills to manage their own responsibilities. For young adults under thirty, this is often the period when core abilities such as: strategic thinking, time management, communication, and emotional control are still forming. If we hand over tasks too early, we risk losing the opportunity to practice, make mistakes, and grow in these areas. The lessons that come from struggling with certain responsibilities, learning to organize them, and discovering personal strengths are irreplaceable. In other words, the discipline required to manage your own tasks is a training ground, and delegating too soon can stunt multidimensional growth.      At the same time, once we have developed sufficient skills, delegation becomes a strategic tool to protect focus and energy. In everyday life, we can start by identifying tasks that do not contribute meaningfully to our development or that consume excessive time and energy without growth. For example, mundane chores, repetitive reporting, or requests that distract from core priorities can be passed to others responsibly. Doing so is not laziness, it is clearing your track so that your effort can be spent on growth, learning, and meaningful challenges. The key is to practice delegation thoughtfully, assessing which responsibilities help you grow and which can be entrusted safely to others.  This balance between personal development and selective delegation is a skill in itself, one that sets the foundation for both freedom and long-term success.)
/E (    However, there is a crucial warning: delegation of responsabilities is not advisable for those who are still developing the essential skills to manage their own responsibilities. For young adults under thirty, this is often the period when core abilities such as: strategic thinking, time management, communication, and emotional control are still forming. If we hand over tasks too early, we risk losing the opportunity to practice, make mistakes, and grow in these areas. The lessons that come from struggling with certain responsibilities, learning to organize them, and discovering personal strengths are irreplaceable. In other words, the discipline required to manage your own tasks is a training ground, and delegating too soon can stunt multidimensional growth.      At the same time, once we have developed sufficient skills, delegation becomes a strategic tool to protect focus and energy. In everyday life, we can start by identifying tasks that do not contribute meaningfully to our development or that consume excessive time and energy without growth. For example, mundane chores, repetitive reporting, or requests that distract from core priorities can be passed to others responsibly. Doing so is not laziness, it is clearing your track so that your effort can be spent on growth, learning, and meaningful challenges. The key is to practice delegation thoughtfully, assessing which responsibilities help you grow and which can be entrusted safely to others.  This balance between personal development and selective delegation is a skill in itself, one that sets the foundation for both freedom and long-term success.)
/Pg 76 0 R
/K [1]
