/P 974 0 R
/E (    Professional athletes embody this principle naturally. A climber scaling a challenging peak, a skydiver jumping from increasing altitudes, or a racecar driver navigating a difficult circuit are all engaging in the discipline of testing their adrenaline. They prepare extensively, follow strict safety protocols, and know their limits but each challenge pushes those limits just enough to grow their skill, awareness, and mental toughness. These experiences illustrate that adrenaline, when disciplined, is a tool for mastery rather than chaos.)
/Pg 79 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1578 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 974 0 R
/E (     Even in everyday life, this practice can be adapted. Trying a new skill that feels intimidating, initiating a difficult conversation, or taking on a leadership role in an unfamiliar environment are all ways to apply the same principle. The goal is not to live permanently in stress or fear, but to periodically test your boundaries so that growth happens outside of comfort zones. Discipline in this context means recognizing the moment you can push yourself safely, preparing for it, and reflecting on the results afterward.  )
/Pg 79 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
