/E (     It is a curious thing how the act of giving and receiving, which seems so natural, can in reality demand as much discipline as waking up before sunrise or completing a demanding task; to give thoughtfully, consistently, and without expectation, and to receive graciously, without pride or hesitation, requires a clarity of intention that most people never consciously cultivate. Imagine a young professional, deeply committed to her work, who chooses each week to spend an hour mentoring a colleague who struggles to find direction not because it will earn her praise, but because she recognizes the subtle power in small, deliberate acts of contribution.)
/Pg 87 0 R
