1688 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 989 0 R
/E (   There comes a point in every disciplined journey when effort alone is no longer enough, and that moment often reveals a truth many people resist accepting: rest is not the opposite of discipline, it is one of its most demanding forms. You know you are truly committed to growth when taking a rest day requires more discipline than pushing through another workout, another late night, or another full calendar. This is where maturity begins, not in how much you can endure, but in how well you know when to stop. To the untrained eye, discipline is a straight line of relentless effort. A hammer striking the same point until the stone yields. But true masters of any craft from the master weaver to the master warrior, from the artist to the astronaut know a secret inscribed in the very pulse of nature: the line of discipline is a waveform. Its peaks of effort are defined, and made possible, by its troughs of restoration. To ignore this rhythm is not strength, it is a fundamental misunderstanding of power. The discipline to rest is not a soft counterpoint to the discipline to work, it is its most intelligent and essential partner. It is the discipline to listen when the body whispers, so it never has to scream.)
/Pg 94 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
