/T (    We become the stories we tell about ourselves, and our friends are often the co-authors. A circle that reminds you of your past failures reinforces an identity of limitation. A circle that affirms your evolving potential helps you author a new, empowered story.       Choosing your circle also teaches self-respect. It communicates, both to yourself and to others, that your time and energy are not unlimited. That you are willing to invest them where growth is mutual and intentional. Over time, this discipline reduces friction in your life. Fewer explanations. Fewer justifications. Less emotional noise. The influence of your circle extends beyond motivation. It affects how you respond to challenges, how you speak to yourself, and how you interpret failure. When surrounded by people who value responsibility, reflection, and consistency, setbacks become lessons rather than reasons to quit. When surrounded by constant complaint, even small difficulties feel overwhelming. Discipline in this context is the courage to protect your mindset before it is shaped for you.       This does not mean every person around you must share identical goals. Diversity of thought enriches growth. What matters is alignment in core values: respect for effort, honesty in feedback, accountability without humiliation, encouragement without pressure. These qualities create an environment where discipline feels supported rather than forced.       My advice: Categorize the people in your life)
