1852 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1014 0 R
/E (   Before we talk about money as a tool, before we discuss discipline, freedom, or responsibility, there is a more uncomfortable question that needs to be asked, one that most people avoid because it forces honesty rather than theory: what relationship do I actually have with money?)
/Pg 119 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1853 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1014 0 R
/E (   Not how much money you make. Not how ambitious you sound when you talk about it. But what money means to you. Whether it creates anxiety or calm whether it feels like security or pressure whether it gives you confidence or exposes fear. Because money, long before it becomes a number, becomes a relationship. For many of us, this relationship was shaped early, without our consent or awareness. It was shaped by how money was discussed at home, by the arguments it caused or the silence surrounding it, by the way adults reacted to bills, savings, success, or lack. Some grew up seeing money as something scarce and fragile, something that could disappear at any moment, creating a mindset of fear and control. Others grew up seeing money as power, as status, as proof of worth, creating a mindset of comparison and performance. Very few were taught to see money as what it truly is: a neutral instrument that reflects discipline, values, and decisions.)
/Pg 119 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
