1916 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1021 0 R
/E (There are two kinds of dungeons. One is visible, imposing, with polished bars. The other is invisible, suffocating, with walls of debt and despair. Succession and Squid Game are two halves of the same broken mirror. In the first, the characters fight for an empire, but inherit a wasteland. In the second, the characters fight for a misery, but sell their souls. And yet, both stories breathe the same poisoned air: that of a world in which money has become the only metric of life. )
/Pg 126 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1917 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1021 0 R
/E (    In Succession, the dungeon is golden. Logan Roy and his children, Kendall, Shiv, Roman, live in a parallel universe of private jets, obscene fortunes and corporate maneuvers worth billions. They possess everything money can buy, but they lack everything money cannot buy: trust, unconditional love, inner peace, true freedom. Every dollar is a brick in the wall that separates them from each other. Their family is not a blood bond; it is a board of directors in a constant struggle for power. Here, money is not a tool for living, but a substitute for life itself. The father's approval, the inheritance of the empire - these are the only currencies that have value.)
/Pg 126 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
