/S /P
/P 1022 0 R
/E (   The characters are so rich that they have completely lost their identity outside of wealth. They are prisoners of excess. They have so many options that they no longer have any authentic choice. All roads lead back to the empire, to the father, to the fight for a crown that brings only loneliness. It is a perfect demonstration of the fact that absolute financial freedom, in the absence of a human purpose, is the most refined form of slavery.)
/Pg 127 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
1921 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1022 0 R
/E (    In Squid Game, the dungeon is made of iron and despair. Here, the characters are not princes of capitalism, but its victims. They do not fight for an empire, but to escape the burden of debt, shame, failure. For Seong Gi-hun and the other players, money does not represent power or inheritance; it represents the only perception of salvation. When survival is in question, ethics becomes a luxury. Human solidarity melts before the instinct of preservation. Childish games become deadly mechanisms, and the most disturbing reality is not the violence, but the ease with which people accept to perpetuate it for a chance at money. This is the captivity of lack. Extreme poverty is as much a prison as extreme wealth. When all you see in front of you is a wall of debt, any loophole, no matter how dark, seems like a way out. You see them leaving their dignity in the locker room, along with their dirty clothes. They are no longer people, but competitors. Money, in this context, is no longer a tool, but a saving obsession that justifies any sacrifice.)
/Pg 127 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
