/E (     Children learn about money long before they understand numbers. They absorb attitudes, reactions, and behaviors from the adults around them. If money is always a source of stress, secrecy, or conflict, children internalize anxiety. If money is treated with clarity, balance, and responsibility, they learn confidence. Financial education does not start with lectures, but with experiences. Simple games, allowances with clear rules, and age-appropriate responsibilities teach more than abstract explanations ever could. When children learn that money is earned, managed, and respected, they develop a healthy relationship with it early on.)
