/S /P
/P 1050 0 R
/E (Take the looming task. Open a blank document or grab a piece of paper. Set a timer for 4 minutes. Write down every single question, thought, step, and fear related to that task in a chaotic, unjudged stream. "Need data from Sarah. Where is last year's file? I don't understand the new template. What's the main point even? I'm afraid it will be rejected. Need to check the guidelines. Should start with an outline. God this is boring...")
/Pg 155 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
2166 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1050 0 R
/E (Stop when the timer goes off. You have just externalized the "monster." It's now outside your head, on paper.)
/Pg 155 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
2167 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1050 0 R
/E (     From that messy list, ask this one question: "What is the very first, smallest, physical movement I could do to make this 1% more real?" Not "start the report." That's the cave. The answer is something like: "Open the folder named 'Q4 Data' on my desktop." Or "Text Sarah: 'Hi, could you please send me the sales figures when you have a moment?'" Or "Write three possible titles at the top of a blank document." This action must be so trivial it feels almost stupid. It should take less than 2 minutes and require no creative thought or major decision-making. This is your entry brick.)
/Pg 155 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
2168 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1050 0 R
/E (     Starting is half the battle. The other half is not stalling again after the first break. For this, you use a visual tracking system that rewards continuation. The "Link the Chain" Method:)
/Pg 155 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
2169 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1050 0 R
/E (Get a simple calendar. Your only goal each day is to "not break the chain" by performing at least one meaningful action on your project. When you do, you put a big, satisfying "X" on that day. The visual chain of X's becomes a motivator in itself. You don't want to be the person who broke a 7-day chain. The motivation shifts from the daunting "Finish the project" to the simple, daily "Protect the chain." The "Parking on a Downhill Slope" Tactic:)
/Pg 155 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
2170 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1050 0 R
/E (Never end a work session at a "natural" stopping point or when you're stuck. Always force yourself to stop in the middle of a sentence, a paragraph, or a simple next step. Write down the exact next sentence you were going to write, or the next sub-task. For example: "[STOPPED HERE. Next: Add the chart from slide 4 to the appendix.]" When you return, you don't face a blank slate or a problem.)
/Pg 155 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
