2227 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1058 0 R
/E (This distinction grants us the profound power to hold an emotion without being held captive by it. We can acknowledge fear without cementing it as cowardice, feel grief without assuming the permanent identity of a victim. Take Abraham Lincoln, who battled lifelong depression while leading a nation through civil war. Or J.K. Rowling, who wrote Harry Potter while unemployed, depressed, and rejected by multiple publishers. What separated these individuals from collapse was not the absence of emotion, but the refusal to let emotion dictate meaning.)
/Pg 163 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
2228 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1058 0 R
/E (    The life of Nelson Mandela stands as the ultimate testament to this principle. For 27 years, imprisoned under a system of brutal injustice, he had every reason to let his identity fuse completely with righteous hatred and bitter resentment. The emotions of outrage and vengeance were not just present; they were justified. He had these feelings.  Yet, he consciously refused to become them. In the crucible of his suffering, he performed the most radical act of self-definition: he separated his core identity from the turbulent emotions he experienced. He chose to host the storm without becoming the storm. Upon his release, he did not emerge as vengeance personified, but as the embodiment of forgiveness and reconciliation. He demonstrated that while we cannot always control what we feel, we hold absolute sovereignty over what we allow those feelings to mean about who we are.)
/Pg 163 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
