2268 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1063 0 R
/E (The wave does not always crash with a roar. Often, it retreats in silence, leaving behind only emotional foam on the shore of consciousness. The nervous system gradually recovers from alert. Breathing deepens, the heart regains its usual rhythm, muscle tension eases. The mind, exhausted from the dramatic effort, begins to clear. Catastrophic thoughts lose their urgency and you begin to see things with some distance, even with a shadow of perspective. Here appears the space between you and the passing emotion. It is perhaps in this space, after the storm has passed, that you can observe the traces left behind: a lesson, a discovered vulnerability, a need to set a boundary, or simply a profound compassion for your own body and soul that have just weathered a storm.)
/Pg 168 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
2269 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1063 0 R
/E (    To know this anatomy is not to nullify the storm. It means knowing that it has a structure, a beginning and an end. It means knowing that in the triggering phase, you can ask: "What exactly touched this alarm bell within me?" In the phase of cognitive amplification, you can interrupt the narrative choir with a simple question: "Is this story fully true?" In the phase of somatic incarnation, you can apply the most direct first aid: a deep, conscious breath that changes the brain's biochemistry. And at the crest, you can hold onto the most important remembrance: this too shall pass.)
/Pg 168 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
2270 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1063 0 R
/E (     Let us never forget that a wave, however large, is still just a movement in water. It is not the water itself. You are the ocean, not the passing wave. To study the anatomy of the wave is to remember your own depth, your own immensity, and your own capacity to contain, with grace and wisdom, any storm that is born and dies within you.)
/Pg 168 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
