2341 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1069 0 R
/E (     Every day we cross invisible borders yet we rarely mark them.  We leave work but we carry the meetings with us, we close the phone but the news keeps echoing in our chest, we step into our homes physically present but emotionally still elsewhere. This is how tension leaks into places that were meant to be safe not because we want it to but because we never learned how to consciously transition. The mind does not reset automatically when the environment changes. If you do not tell your nervous system that one chapter has ended it will assume the danger pressure or urgency is still active. That is why anger from the office spills into conversations that have nothing to do with work and why anxiety from endless scrolling shows up as irritability silence or exhaustion at home. Rituals exist to give the brain a clear signal that something is over and something else is beginning. Even simple acts can become powerful when done with intention. A short pause before opening the door, a deep breath where you imagine placing the day outside, a physical gesture like washing your hands and mentally releasing what no longer belongs to you. These small moments create psychological closure.)
/Pg 174 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
2342 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1069 0 R
/E (    Without rituals emotions accumulate, they stack on top of each other until everything feels heavy and overwhelming. When you consciously transition you are not denying what you felt you are honoring it and choosing not to let it define the next space you enter. )
/Pg 174 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
