/E (    Lisa Feldman Barrett explain that the brain is a prediction machine constantly categorizing sensations and that emotions are not fixed reactions but constructions based on context and language, which means changing the label can literally change the experience itself. Literature offers powerful mirrors of this process in Dune Frank Herbert writes that fear is the mind killer not because fear exists but because the meaning assigned to it paralyzes action while Buddhist philosophy teaches that naming an emotion gently creates distance between the observer and the experience allowing the wave to pass without drowning us. Relabeling does not say nothing is happening, it says something is happening and I choose how to understand it and this cognitive shift has a physiological effect.)
/Pg 176 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
