/E (View your network not as a series of bilateral friendships, but as a portfolio of human connections. A wise portfolio manager diversifies and rarely liquidates an entire asset class in a panic. They might reduce a position, rebalance, or hold through volatility, knowing that today's underperformer might be tomorrow's lifesaver. The colleague you find difficult today might be the only one who understands a niche skill you need in five years. The client you parted ways with amicably might rise to lead a company you want to work for. The ex-partner you treated with respect might become an invaluable co-parent or a voice of unexpected support during a crisis. You are not just managing relationships for today. You are managing the connective tissue of your future. The person who masters the graceful exit, who chooses the quiet fade over the scorched earth, understands a profound truth: in a long life, the world has a mysterious way of becoming very small, and people have an unpredictable way of reappearing. Leave the bridges standing. Your future self, stranded on an unfamiliar shore, will look to the horizon and be grateful to see not the smoke of your past anger, but the faint, enduring outline of a path that still remains.)
/Pg 209 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
