10478 0 obj
<<
/Title (The Power That Endures      Enduring power is not loud, fast, or immediately visible. It is built quietly through consistency, through showing up even when results are not obvious. This type of power does not rely on motivation, because motivation fades. It relies on systems, routines, and the acceptance that growth is a long-term process.      I will win not immediately but definitely reflects a mindset rooted in patience and certainty. It belongs to people who understand that progress compounds over time, and that pressure is not something to escape, but something to work through. This is the kind of power that cannot be rushed, but once built, it is difficult to break.      A powerful example is Andy Dufresne from The Shawshank Redemption. He does not fight the system impulsively or seek quick victories. Instead, he endures years of pressure, isolation, and limitation while maintaining discipline, clarity, and inner structure. His strength is not visible at first, but it is steady, intentional, and ultimately unstoppable. Pressure does not weaken him; it sharpens him.      This is why pressure creates power when approached correctly. It forces growth, demands structure, and removes illusions. Over time, discipline becomes identity, and identity becomes resilience. At that point, strength is no longer something you try to access; it becomes something you carry with you, regardless of circumstances.)
/Dest [31 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 10474 0 R
/Next 10480 0 R
>>
