10604 0 obj
<<
/Title (From this example, we can understand that discipline is not always about comfort. For those in environments where behavior is structured by expectation, discipline sometimes feels like internal negotiation between who we are and what is expected of us. The British monarchy teaches that consistency in roles, responsibility and protocol can shape actions, public perception, and identity over a lifetime, regardless of how welcomed or resisted those structures are by the individuals themselves.     Different cultures have their own models for discipline that influence everyday life. In many East Asian societies, such as in Japan, discipline is expressed through respect, precision, and community responsibility. Daily rituals from school cleaning duties to collective punctuality are not only about maintaining order, but also about teaching respect, teamwork, and mutual care. These culturally embedded practices show how discipline can shape character and social cohesion naturally, not through punishment, but through shared responsibility.)
/Dest [78 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 10600 0 R
/Next 10606 0 R
>>
endobj
